```markdown
---
description: Leases as facts under the JIT authority pattern — an advisory
holder proposes, an owning grantor applies at safe points, and the negotiation
is status-as-data.
---

# DAO LEASE

Status: proposed design target, derived from and subordinate to
[`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). Where this document and the foundations
disagree, the foundations win. Transport specifications may define how lease
facts are carried; they may not redefine what those facts mean.

## Purpose

A lease bounds how long a grant remains a reason to retain a resource or
continue a condition when the holder may disappear without releasing it. It
solves the absence of future evidence: a holder that dies, hangs, or partitions
away stops proving itself interested, and that silence is the reclaim
condition. It does not detect death, prove reachability, authorize anyone,
cancel an operation, or make a resource available.

There is no lease object, no method on one, and no background manager behind
one. A lease is facts, accreted on an ordinary stream; lapse is an
interpretation of those facts against the grantor's clock; and the reclaim is
an act of the grantor's composition, recorded as a fact.

## The pattern adopted

This document is an instance of the authority pattern
[`jit-design.md`](./jit-design.md) establishes, applied to a time-bounded
resource rather than a running program:

| jit-design | lease |
|---|---|
| execution emits trace datoms | the holder emits renewal evidence datoms |
| JIT observes, advisory only | the holder requests; only grantor-authored facts establish terms |
| VM applies at safe points it owns | the grantor accepts, refuses, judges, reclaims at safe points it owns |
| `:proposed/:accepted/:rejected/:rolled-back` | `:requested/:granted/:refused/:released` + `:reclaimed` act |
| guard failure emits deopt; fall back to baseline | lapse judged; reclaim appended; resource returns to unleased baseline |

The structure, and its denials, are inherited: no callbacks, no mutable side
channel, no mutation by the advisory side, no application except at the
owner's own points, and the whole negotiation as queryable data.

## Authority and safe points

Two parties, one owning each half.

**The holder is advisory.** Every holder-authored fact is a proposal or a
statement of the holder's own interest. It never establishes a term. A renewal
request does not extend anything; only the grantor's acceptance does.

**The grantor owns application.** The grantor alone mints the grant, accepts
or refuses renewals, judges lapse, and reclaims. Authority follows the
resource: whoever is composed with the thing to be reclaimed is the grantor,
and no consensus with the holder is needed.

The grantor applies at **safe points it already owns** — each run of its
driver — and never upon receipt of a holder fact. A fact arriving is data; what
it means is decided when the grantor next runs. The deadline of a term is a
predicate the grantor evaluates at those runs against its own clock, not a
scheduled interrupt and not a structural boundary. This is the JIT safe-point
rule with the structural points replaced by a cadence and a time predicate.

## A lease is facts, accreted

A lease is identified by a plain-data `:dao.lease/lease` value, fresh for
every new grant, so delayed facts from a former grant cannot touch a later
one. A term is identified by a plain-data `:dao.lease/term` value, fresh for
every accepted term; each names the term it succeeds.

Every transition is a datom `[e a v t m]`. `t` orders the fact in the medium;
it is not a lease clock and never an expiration time. Provenance and source
scope belong to the interpreter. The current status of a term is the most
recent valid status fact the judge has observed — the same "latest accepted
transition" reading the patch status gets, never a mutable field.

## Vocabulary

All statuses live under the single attribute `:dao.lease/status`.

**Grant** — grantor-authored, establishes the first term:

```clojure
[L :dao.lease/status     :granted]
[L :dao.lease/holder     H]
[L :dao.lease/subject    S]
[L :dao.lease/term       T1]
[L :dao.lease/duration-ms D]
```

The term begins when the grantor accepts the grant in its own clock domain. A
grant is a maximum period during which the lease may remain a reason to
continue, never a guarantee the subject stays available.

**Renewal request** — holder-authored, advisory:

```clojure
[L :dao.lease/status     :requested]
[L :dao.lease/request    R]
[L :dao.lease/prior-term T1]
[L :dao.lease/duration-ms D]     ; requested; optional
```

It names the term the holder believes current and expresses continued
interest. It extends nothing.

**Accepted renewal** — grantor-authored, establishes the next term:

```clojure
[L :dao.lease/status     :granted]
[L :dao.lease/term       T2]
[L :dao.lease/prior-term T1]
[L :dao.lease/request    R]
[L :dao.lease/duration-ms D]     ; accepted; may be shorter than requested
```

Only this fact establishes T2. The accepted duration may be shortened; silence,
transport acceptance, and receipt of the request are not acceptance. The term
starts at the grantor's acceptance instant — it does not add time to a former
deadline, so early requests cannot build an unbounded future obligation.

**Refusal** — grantor-authored:

```clojure
[L :dao.lease/status  :refused]
[L :dao.lease/request R]
```

**Release** — holder-authored, terminal for the holder's interest: *I am done
early; you may reclaim now.* Courtesy, not obligation; a system in which no
release ever arrives is correct and merely slower.

```clojure
[L :dao.lease/status :released]
```

**Reclaim** — grantor-authored, the deopt analog: the grantor judged lapse or
honored a release and acted. This is the fallback-to-baseline made visible.

```clojure
[L :dao.lease/status :reclaimed]
[L :dao.lease/term   T]
[L :dao.lease/why    :lapsed]    ; or :released, or :policy
```

The append records the act; it is never the act itself, and nobody is obliged
to read it.

## Lapse is a judgment; reclaim is the act

Nothing fires when a lease lapses. Lapse is a judgment value an interpreter
computes — `:live | :lapsed | :released | :unknown` — not a fact in the trace.
It is the lease's guard failure: the time-guard failed, and the grantor falls
back to baseline by reclaiming. Two interpreters with different tolerances may
legitimately disagree; possession resolves the disagreement, not truth — the
interpreter composed with the resource is the one whose judgment becomes a
reclaim.

A judgment may be appended as an observation for other consumers. Such a fact
means *this interpreter judged lapse from its stated perspective*; it is not a
universal event and does not replace the underlying facts.

## Whose clock decides

No absolute time crosses a boundary. A duration is the only time-bearing value,
and a renewal request carries none of its own authority. The grantor's clock
decides because the grantor owns the thing to be reclaimed; a holder's clock
cannot impose a deadline on another host.

The grantor judges on a **monotonic** elapsed-time source. Wall-clock
adjustments must not extend or shorten a live term. For an accepted term:

```
deadline = acceptance-instant + accepted-duration + grantor-tolerance
```

Tolerance is grantor policy and does not alter the accepted duration. Network
delay consumes the term from the holder's view, so a holder that needs
continuous validity renews early enough to cover transit and processing delay —
the duration is the outer bound of its safety, never its cadence. The clocks
still never meet.

## Judging lapse

An interpreter that would conclude lapse reads the medium through its own
cursor and keeps, as private interpreter state, the current accepted term, its
acceptance instant on the grantor's monotonic clock, and the grant's policy.
That state is derived and rebuildable — the moral equivalent of a cursor, never
a registry.

It validates provenance and causality first: only grantor-authorized grants and
acceptances, holder-authorized requests and releases. It selects the current
term along the `:dao.lease/prior-term` chain; a stale or forking request is
history, not a competing present.

A valid release observed means `:released`. A current reading at or beyond the
deadline means `:lapsed`. Before the deadline, `:live`. If the judge lacks a
trustworthy current term or the grantor's acceptance instant — after a gap or a
restart — the judgment is `:unknown`. Absence of evidence after a gap is not
evidence of lapse.

Acceptance and judgment are serialized in one grantor-owned interpretation
order: one operational answer, without claiming a global order.

## Advisory holder, obligated grantor

The holder remains advisory even though its inaction is consequential: not
renewing is simply the holder's own interest ending, and the grantor reclaims.

The grantor is the one whose inaction would leak, so the lease names the
obligation and its carrier: **the composition that owns the resource must drive
the judge at a declared cadence**, so that a lapse becomes a reclaim within the
composition's chosen bound. This is the same cadence a reader's runtime already
carries when it retries `:dao.stream/blocked`. The lease installs no timer and
hands no callback to a scheduler; it states the obligation, and the driver
composition bears it. Reclamation is eventually-timely, never instantaneous —
and no regression, since an object design's expired leases are likewise
enforced only when the grantor notices.

## Retention, gaps, restart

Lease facts obey the medium's retention like every other fact. A lease never
pins a cursor, delays eviction, or converts bounded retention into
reader-waiting retention.

A judge that has already accepted a term may keep judging from its own state
after the source fact is evicted. A gap before all relevant facts are
incorporated makes the state `:unknown`; the newest retained renewal is not a
complete history, and the judge must not treat it as one. Each composition
declares its `:unknown` policy — a finite server resource ordinarily reclaims
and requires a fresh grant, because missing history cannot justify indefinite
retention.

A duration-only trace cannot reconstruct live validity after the grantor loses
its acceptance instant. A restart therefore does one of three explicit things:
restore a grantor-local checkpoint in the same clock domain, classify existing
leases `:unknown` and regrant, or classify them `:unknown` and reclaim. It must
not restart each retained duration from replay time; replay is not renewal.

## Policy and hostile holders

Every grantor sets a maximum accepted duration, and may also set a maximum
consecutive leased interval, a renewal count, an aggregate lifetime, and
request-rate limits. These are explicit inputs to the grantor interpreter; no
universal aggregate exists, because some healthy relationships are meant to
renew indefinitely.

A hostile holder can request forever, and a request grants nothing. If a
grantor accepts renewals forever, that is the grantor's declared policy, not a
power the holder holds. Lease identity is not authentication or authorization:
provenance and capability checking belong to the boundary, and a lease is never
a substitute for Shibi.

## What a lease may never do

A lease never carries absolute time across a boundary; never trusts a
holder-supplied timestamp; never lets a request extend itself; never renews
itself, installs a thread, registers a callback, or hides a scheduler; never
invokes reclamation from the judge; never suppresses a gap or treats missing
history as a complete trace; never proves a holder dead; never creates
ownership, authorization, mutual exclusion, or consensus; and never stands in
for cancellation of an operation whose result is still pending.

## Out of scope

What is leasable, and how a grant is requested before the first term, are the
domain's business. Transport framing and encoding are carriage. Probe
selection, ping cadence, renewal margin, retry and backoff are composition
policy. The resource action taken after lapse or release is out of scope.
Authentication, authorization, capability issuance, distributed clock
synchronization, consensus, and exactly-once delivery are out of scope.

## Seam to DaoStream and WebSocket

DaoStream requires no lease operation and no lease-aware surface. Lease facts
are ordinary stream elements; lease interpreters are ordinary interpreters.

[`dao.stream.ws.md`](./dao.stream.ws.md) owns carriage only: which data or
control frames carry a renewal request, and the close code an endpoint uses
when it reclaims a connection — a sibling of the ended-stream code. The WebSocket
transport defines no lease vocabulary, chooses no duration or tolerance, owns no
cadence, and never judges.

The serving composition is the lease's **VM**: it is the applier. It supplies
the durations, caps, and tolerance; drives the judge; and acts on its
judgments. The lease document owns meaning, the transport document owns wire
representation, and the composition owns policy and execution — the same
three-way split `jit-design.md` draws between the trace protocol, the patch
stream, and the VM that applies it.
```

# 4. The parallel confirms the seam; it does not move it

`jit-design.md` puts the trace protocol in the VM and the patch stream outside
it — the *owner of execution* is the applier, and the *observer* is outside. The
lease follows the same shape and thereby *pins* where things live rather than
relocating them:

- **Lease vocabulary** (statuses, clock discipline, judgment rules, safe-point
  rule) lives in `dao.lease.md` — the analog of the trace protocol living in the
  VM. It is datom.world-level, not DaoStream-level.
- **The applier is the serving composition**, not the transport — the analog of
  the VM. For WebSocket this is the v2 plan's 4c composition: it owns durations,
  caps, cadence, and the reclaim decision.
- **`dao.stream.ws.md` is pure carriage** — the analog of the wire, not of either
  party. Renewal requests travel as ordinary `:ws/payload` values on the socket
  the holder already writes; the reclaim reaches the wire as the transport's
  close code.

One refinement over the previous draft's seam, forced by the pattern's
judgment/act split: the ws close code is the *reclaim's* wire trace, and the
decision to reclaim is the serving composition's. The ws document must not grow
a "lapse detection" role — that would put the applier inside the transport,
which is exactly the collapse the pattern forbids. So the answer to the
question is: no, the vocabulary does not move; the pattern gives the existing
seam a named precedent and makes the composition's applier role explicit.

